Dagster automatically infers `raw_transactions → cleaned_transactions → fraud_model` as the asset graph. The Dagster UI shows this lineage visually, with each asset's last materialization time — when `fraud_model`'s performance seems off, an engineer can immediately see in the UI when `cleaned_transactions` was last materialized and trace back to `raw_transactions`, quickly spotting that the upstream table hasn't refreshed in 3 days due to an unrelated database issue.

```python
from metaflow import FlowSpec, step, batch

class TrainFlow(FlowSpec):
    @step
    def start(self):
        self.data = load_data()
        self.next(self.train)

    @batch(cpu=4, gpu=1, memory=16000)
    @step
    def train(self):
        self.model = fit_model(self.data)
        self.next(self.end)

    @step
    def end(self):
        print("done")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    TrainFlow()
```
A data scientist runs `python trainflow.py run` locally on a small sample to debug `start` and `train`; once working, running the same script with the full dataset automatically executes the `@batch`-decorated `train` step on AWS Batch with a GPU, while `start`/`end` still run locally — no code rewrite needed to scale the compute-intensive step.
